{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "subsection_title", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\subsection{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{guide-subsection-toric}\n\n\\noindent\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7e50e445235c0f5cf85fc6c80e92fa3e8b3839467aa756b5cc86e4f5c5645a2b", "source_text": "Toric stacks", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e19c9bb3c69b8e98c36065f6b38ef230bdc74c0ecd0eacf1a5fd140d994555d1", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:title"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\begin{itemize}\n"}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:25af7dbc0dd9f36c69de23f96b514016a82878a24284fd6d62ba07a2e777db7e", "source_text": "Toric stacks provide a great class of examples and a natural testing ground for conjectures due to the dictionary between the geometry of a toric stack and the combinatorics of its stacky fan in a similar way that toric varieties provide examples and counterexamples in scheme theory.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5d431b2fd411064b66baab4dea85a31a13e1b4ccb3f3af9e49f31c07fed5d3cf", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:p001"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Borisov, Chen and Smith: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{bcs}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e655f41300ba499a46648b37b37a80a09b356cf806b9fbb67a18d4a685f6a529", "source_text": "The orbifold Chow ring of toric Deligne-Mumford stacks", "source_text_hash": "sha256:1384c604dd2209251661933c8a997144e816c800272440801eabd6881c4f109c", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item001"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {"EMPHCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMPHOPEN_0001": "\\emph{"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:765e1caf5df55067936bf835026a4e1d7be729f60a442fd4e509cd18c2daaa17", "source_text": "Inspired by Cox's construction for toric varieties, this paper defines smooth toric DM stacks as explicit quotient stacks associated to a combinatorial object called a <EMPHOPEN_0001>stacky fan<EMPHCLOSE_0001>.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d0b0d3d18634be5def7b40625e20858142afe8062f83d7dcc96e1532d65dfb87", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item001-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Iwanari: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{iwanari_toric}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1e5bf98d99ce29c4bcde27baf00407d54516aec868971fe37636430a72e6276d", "source_text": "The category of toric stacks", "source_text_hash": "sha256:bb71e1cd210599581cae72c6b02268345107a66abf850f4e0e9c08221f9b91a1", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item002"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {"CITE_0001": "\\cite{bcs}", "EMPHCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMPHOPEN_0001": "\\emph{", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathbf{G}_m \\hookrightarrow \\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{X} \\times \\mathbf{G}_m \\rightarrow \\mathcal{X}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:22520e12965104a1cfef3c0df4a89c2aa787b76899c98e0621f8177a33896297", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:6e5344a8a19ef46e9805bcb9bdf9e1e2a959e11b408515fbf8ce83d375aed732", "source_text": "This paper defines a <EMPHOPEN_0001>toric triple<EMPHCLOSE_0001> as a smooth Deligne-Mumford stack <MATH_0001> with an open immersion <MATH_0002> with dense image (and therefore <MATH_0003> is an orbifold) and an action <MATH_0004>. It is shown that there is an equivalence between the 2-category of toric triples and the 1-category of stacky fans. The relationship between toric triples and the definition of smooth toric DM stacks in <CITE_0001> is discussed.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:411cd3e2da6660fbc242b584f421be56f62a9d1fc20610c3d7bd6c16ac4eb20e", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item002-quote"}
