{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {"LATEX_0001": "``", "LATEX_0002": "''", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{T}$", "MATH_0003": "$T \\times BG$", "MATH_0004": "$G$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{T}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:13f8822ebbe4fe969b1270e93cee703a5f4c6b8d65967bbaf4706c81df071c7c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:cb673bb5841f9543f9359794408bfd5b222db71ca9df8dd71ab47a2177106cfe", "source_text": "This paper defines a smooth toric DM stack as a smooth DM stack <MATH_0001> with the action of a DM torus <MATH_0002> (ie. a Picard stack isomorphic to <MATH_0003> with <MATH_0004> finite) having an open dense orbit isomorphic to <MATH_0005>. They give a <LATEX_0001>bottom-up description<LATEX_0002> and prove an equivalence between smooth toric DM stacks and stacky fans.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:8738ed4f515e509dd4b2c5b6092ac2b60d92717e1085a6cea13af4918bd25086", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item005-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Geraschenko and Satriano: \\emph{", "suffix": "}\n\\cite{gs_toric1} and \\cite{gs_toric2}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:c884388364a47d93082b2b15aa0d6323a01d9a09023659fc01b8dc3f75505767", "source_text": "Toric Stacks I and II", "source_text_hash": "sha256:806c5e80dabcf54a278f18e3a3cc2556322e671765d3478294c13c98e5dcbb15", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item006"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04V5", "placeholders": {"CITE_0001": "\\cite{perroni}"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:21da65bea2c6ad15a13b54030818bc503584bb97f3d5f1bdbcaff6d3bcc582ee", "source_text": "These papers define a toric stack as the stack quotient of a toric variety by a subgroup of its torus. A generically stacky toric stack is defined as a torus invariant substack of a toric stack. This definition encompasses and extends previous definitions of toric stacks. The first paper develops a dictionary between the combinatorics of stacky fans and the geometry of the corresponding stacks. It also gives a moduli interpretation of smooth toric stacks, generalizing the one in <CITE_0001>. The second paper proves an intrinsic characterization of toric stacks.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:88309fe00c3e2a1ce0644c0b8c0a890bd155716a721900c0cf05e4e7fd57544c", "unit_id": "tag:04V5:item006-quote"}
